# Target C-II — two-anchor peeling plus parallel outside pairs forces a `1.1812D` exact-flex reservoir

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only structural strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** canonical full-core stopped-train system; terminal FREE constants stated below  
**Depends on:** audited stopped-train decomposition; strict convexity of bounded nonquadrilateral contact faces; exact train-angle closure coordinates; two-anchor elimination; terminal correlated stability; canonical mixed rigidity / live diameter locking  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_free_terminal_two_anchor_pair_shield_flex_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The two-anchor theorem previously had an all-or-nothing endpoint. Complete activation
integrated essentially all train angles, while a stall left an internal degree-three
residual core with no quantitative use of the already exposed variables.

There is a stronger exact decomposition. Freeze the residual-internal train system and
keep the already activated faces solved by the same triangular pivot Jacobian. At a
residual face with two trains going to the outer/activated side, maximality forces those
two terminal side directions to be projectively parallel. Their oriented unit vectors
are exact negatives, so a common rotation of the two train angles preserves the
residual-face closure identically. If either train ends on an activated face, the old
pivot system absorbs that change by the implicit-function theorem.

The resulting finite bound is

\[
\boxed{
 m_D\ge {b-f\over2}
}
\tag{PS.1}
\]

whenever the two-anchor process stalls. More precisely, the gap above `(b-f)/2`
records exactly how far the residual system is from the worst one-anchor normal form.
In the current terminal FREE window this gives

\[
\boxed{
 \liminf {m_D\over D}>1.1812298040,
}
\tag{PS.2}
\]

and in the small-complement / near-maximal-detour branch

\[
\boxed{
 \liminf {m_D\over D}>1.1984798040.
}
\tag{PS.3}
\]

Thus the previous residual 3-core is not an escape from a large exact-flex manifold.
At the counting endpoint it must sharpen to a one-anchor internal 4-core.

---

## 1. Maximal two-anchor peeling

Let `O` be the actual outer face and let `mathcal F` be the `f` bounded
nonquadrilateral contact faces. Run the preceding two-anchor activation process to
maximality. Let

\[
 A\subseteq\mathcal F,
 \qquad U:=\mathcal F\setminus A,
 \qquad a_F:=|A|,
 \qquad u:=|U|=f-a_F.
\tag{1.1}
\]

The complete-activation case `U=empty` is treated in Section 5. Assume for now that
`U` is nonempty.

For `F in U`, let `b_U(F)` be the number of incident stopped trains whose other
terminal face is `O` or a face in `A`. Maximality says that all such side directions
at `F` span at most one projective line. Strict convexity allows at most two polygon
sides in one projective direction. Hence

\[
 b_U(F)\in\{0,1,2\}.
\tag{1.2}
\]

Write

\[
 n_j:=\#\{F\in U:b_U(F)=j\},
 \qquad j=0,1,2.
\tag{1.3}
\]

Thus

\[
 u=n_0+n_1+n_2,
 \qquad
 d_U:=\sum_{F\in U}(2-b_U(F))=2n_0+n_1.
\tag{1.4}
\]

For each exceptional face put `x(F)=deg(F)-5>=0`, and write

\[
 x_A:=\sum_{F\in A}x(F),
 \qquad
 x_U:=\sum_{F\in U}x(F).
\tag{1.5}
\]

Then `x_A+x_U=Q-f`.

---

## 2. Freeze the residual core and keep every exposed angle

Let `I_U` be the number of complete trains incident to at least one residual face, and
let `E(U)` be the number with both bounded endpoints in `U`. Put

\[
 B_U:=\sum_{F\in U}b_U(F)=2u-d_U.
\]

The residual transport degree sum gives

\[
 2E(U)+B_U=5u+x_U.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Since `I_U=E(U)+B_U`,

\[
\boxed{
 I_U={5u+x_U+B_U\over2}.
}
\tag{2.2}
\]

First freeze every train incident to `U`. For each activated face retain its two
recorded pivot train angles as dependent variables. All other non-residual train
angles are free.

No pivot train is incident to `U`: when its face was activated, the pivot's other
endpoint was `O` or an earlier activated face. Therefore the old pivot Jacobian is
unchanged after the residual train columns are frozen. Ordered by activation time it
is block upper triangular with invertible `2x2` diagonal blocks. The implicit-function
theorem solves all `2a_F` pivots smoothly as functions of the remaining free angles.
Residual face closures are unchanged and strict rhombi close identically.

This gives an exact finite contact-preserving manifold of dimension

\[
 m_{peel}=T-I_U-2a_F.
\tag{2.3}
\]

Because `U` is nonempty, at least one residual-internal train is frozen: every residual
face has total degree at least five and at most two outside incidences, hence at least
three U--U incidences. Thus the all-train rotation vector is not in this manifold.
After fixing translation, (2.3) is already a non-Euclidean dimension.

Using

\[
 T=(q-k)+2f,
 \qquad
 B_U=2u-d_U,
 \qquad
 f=u+a_F,
\]

we get

\[
 m_{peel}
 =(q-k)-{3u+x_U\over2}+{d_U\over2}.
\tag{2.4}
\]

The loaded-hull identity `b+Q=2(q-k)` then gives the equivalent exact form

\[
\boxed{
 m_{peel}
 ={b-2f\over2}
 +{3a_F+x_A+d_U\over2}.
}
\tag{2.5}
\]

---

## 3. Every two-outside residual face supplies one exact pair coordinate

Fix `F in U` with `b_U(F)=2`. Let the two outside trains have oriented unit side
vectors `v_1,v_2` on the cyclic boundary of `F`.

Maximality of the activation process makes their projective directions equal. On a
strictly convex polygon, two sides in one projective direction have opposite oriented
directions. Since both lengths are one,

\[
 v_2=-v_1.
\tag{3.1}
\]

Give both complete trains the same angle change `theta`. Their contribution to the
closure of `F` remains

\[
 R_\theta v_1+R_\theta v_2=0
\tag{3.2}
\]

for every sufficiently small `theta`; all other incident residual trains remain fixed.
Hence the residual closure equation is preserved exactly.

The other endpoint of each selected train is either `O` or a face in `A`.

- If it is `O`, there is no second bounded exceptional-face equation.
- If it lies in `A`, the changed train angle enters an activated-face closure equation.
  Treat `theta` as an additional free parameter. The same invertible pivot Jacobian
  from Section 2 then solves all activated-face pivots as smooth functions of these
  pair parameters as well as the original non-residual free angles.

A U--A train can never itself be one of those pivots, because its residual endpoint was
not active when the A-end face entered the activation order.

Strict rhombi still close identically under the selected train rotations, including
when one selected train is singleton. Different residual faces use disjoint outside
trains, so the `n_2` pair parameters are independent. They were frozen in the initial
peeling manifold, hence they add `n_2` exact dimensions:

\[
\boxed{
 m_D\ge m_{peel}+n_2.
}
\tag{3.3}
\]

This is the key point: a parallel outside pair remains usable even when one or both
trains terminate on already activated bounded faces. The triangular pivot system
absorbs those secondary closure changes.

---

## 4. Exact finite lower bound and the one-anchor normal form

Insert `d_U=2n_0+n_1` into (2.5) and then use (3.3):

\[
\begin{aligned}
 m_D
 &\ge {b-2f\over2}
 +{3a_F+x_A\over2}
 +n_0+{n_1\over2}+n_2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.1}
\]

Since

\[
 f=a_F+n_0+n_1+n_2,
\]

(4.1) rearranges exactly to

\[
\boxed{
 m_D
 \ge
 {b-f\over2}
 +a_F+{x_A\over2}+{n_0+n_2\over2}.
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

Therefore every stalled activation has the finite universal bound

\[
\boxed{
 m_D\ge {b-f\over2}.
}
\tag{4.3}
\]

Equation (4.2) also identifies the unique counting normal form that can approach this
floor. A near-minimizer must have, up to its permitted error budget,

\[
 a_F\approx0,
 \qquad
 x_A\approx0,
 \qquad
 n_0+n_2\approx0.
\tag{4.4}
\]

Thus almost every residual face must have exactly one train to the outer/activated
side. Since every exceptional face has degree at least five, the residual induced
B--B transport graph then has internal degree at least four at almost every face.
The old generic internal 3-core obstruction has sharpened to an asymptotic
**one-anchor internal 4-core** at the exact-flex endpoint.

No pentagon concentration or rhombic-fullness hypothesis is needed for (4.2)--(4.3).

---

## 5. Complete activation versus stalled activation

If `U` is empty, the preceding full two-anchor elimination theorem gives

\[
 m_D=(q-k)-1.
\tag{5.1}
\]

If `U` is nonempty, (4.3) gives `(b-f)/2`. Hence the finite stopped-train system has

\[
\boxed{
 m_D\ge
 \begin{cases}
 (q-k)-1,&U=\varnothing,\\[2mm]
 (b-f)/2,&U\ne\varnothing.
 \end{cases}}
\tag{5.2}
\]

This removes the residual degree-three core as a low-dimensional flex escape.

---

## 6. Live terminal FREE window: `1.1812298040D`

Use the correlated terminal variables

\[
 \xi:=\lim\left({\tau_D\over D}-{\pi\over2}-g\right),
 \qquad
 r:=\lim{Q-f\over D},
 \qquad
 \delta:=\lim{b-P_F\over D},
\]

where

\[
 g=0.007707,
 \qquad
 \xi,r,\delta\ge0,
\]

and, under

\[
 \limsup t_D\le\mu_*+\varepsilon,
\]

\[
\boxed{
 \xi+2(r+\delta)\le5\varepsilon.
}
\tag{6.1}
\]

Put

\[
 M_*:=4\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}+2g.
\tag{6.2}
\]

The exact terminal ledger gives

\[
 {f\over D}
 ={M_*+2\xi-\delta-r\over5}+o(1),
 \qquad
 {b\over D}=\pi+\delta+o(1).
\tag{6.3}
\]

For a stalled activation, substitute (6.3) into (4.3):

\[
\boxed{
 \liminf {m_D\over D}
 \ge
 {\pi\over2}-{M_*\over10}
 +{3\over5}\delta
 +{1\over10}r
 -{1\over5}\xi.
}
\tag{6.4}
\]

Only `xi` has a negative coefficient. Under (6.1), the lower bound is minimized by

\[
 \delta=r=0,
 \qquad
 \xi=5\varepsilon.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
 \liminf {m_D\over D}
 \ge
 c_{pair}(\varepsilon)
 :={\pi\over2}-{M_*\over10}-\varepsilon.
}
\tag{6.5}
\]

At the current live endpoint `epsilon_0=0.00710`, the exact-rational certificate proves

\[
\boxed{
 c_{pair}(0.00710)>1.1812298040.
}
\tag{6.6}
\]

The complete-activation alternative (5.1) is much larger, because the certified
terminal `t` floor is `1.9603028495...`. Thus (6.6) is unconditional throughout the
stated live window.

Applying the existing live diameter-locking theorem to this exact manifold gives

\[
\boxed{
 \operatorname{rank}G_D,
 \ |M_{flex\to H}|,
 \ I_+,
 \ I_-
 \ge(1.1812298040-o(1))D.
}
\tag{6.7}
\]

Hence the total signed flex/diameter derivative-incidence count is at least

\[
\boxed{(2.3624596080-o(1))D.}
\tag{6.8}
\]

---

## 7. Near-maximal-detour branch: `1.1984798040D`

In the small-complement branch of the live complement/detour theorem,

\[
 \delta>0.01725,
 \qquad
 \xi+2r\le0.001.
\tag{7.1}
\]

The right side of (6.4) is minimized on the closed relaxation by

\[
 \delta=0.01725,
 \qquad
 r=0,
 \qquad
 \xi=0.001.
\]

The exact-rational certificate therefore gives the safe closed floor

\[
\boxed{
 \liminf {m_D\over D}>1.1984798040.
}
\tag{7.2}
\]

The scalar inequality is actually strict because `delta>0.01725`.

---

## 8. Remaining obstruction

This still does not close C-II. It changes the active obstruction substantially.
Every terminal survivor in the live window now carries more than `1.1812D` exact
contact-flex dimensions, and the near-maximal-detour branch carries more than
`1.1984D`. The actual diameter system must positively lock that entire manifold with
at least the same number of pairwise distinct diameter bars and with both derivative
signs in every flex coordinate.

If the finite lower bound is nearly sharp, (4.2) gives a more specific object than the
old residual 3-core: almost no face activates, almost every residual face has exactly
one outside train, and the residual B--B graph is internally at least 4-valent at almost
every vertex. That one-anchor 4-core is now the clean combinatorial obstruction to the
full `(q-k)-1` train manifold.

The next useful theorem should attack either side directly:

1. show that the straight-line diameter thrackle cannot positively lock the explicit
   parallel-pair / peeled coordinates at rank `>1.1812D`; or
2. use the one-anchor 4-core to find another exact train-angle elimination or a
   source-preserving finite diameter descent.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** proved the exact peeling-plus-pair inequality (4.2), the finite stalled
floor `(b-f)/2`, the live `1.1812298040D` exact-flex / locking floor, the
near-maximal-detour `1.1984798040D` refinement, and the one-anchor 4-core endpoint
normal form. C-II remains open.